# Map Guide 2 · Part A India. Same fields as map_guide_content.py; x is now a full explanation:
# what the place or body IS, why it matters, and the connected facts the exam asks around it.
P = lambda n, g, x, **kw: dict(n=n, g=g, x=x, **kw)

CH_INDIA = [
dict(id="passes", part="India", t="Himalayan passes and borders", intro=(
 "A pass (in Tibetan and Ladakhi, a 'la') is a low gap in a mountain range that people can walk or drive through. "
 "For thousands of years traders, pilgrims and armies crossed the Himalaya only at these gaps, so each pass is tied to a route, a border and a story. "
 "Read the passes from west to east, the way India's northern border runs. In Ladakh and Kashmir they lead towards Pakistan-held land and Xinjiang. "
 "In Himachal and Uttarakhand they are old trade and pilgrim routes into Tibet. In Sikkim and Arunachal they face China across the Line of Actual Control (LAC), the de facto border that is not agreed on a map. "
 "The exam's favourite trick is to pair a pass with the wrong state, so learn each pass together with its state and with the road or tunnel that now goes over or under it."), P=[
 P("Karakoram Pass", [77.83, 35.51], "The highest pass on the old Silk Road (about 5,540 m), between Ladakh and Xinjiang in China. Caravans of horses and yaks once carried silk, tea and wool over it between Leh and Yarkand. Today it is closed to trade. It lies just east of the Siachen Glacier and close to Daulat Beg Oldi, India's highest airstrip. Do not confuse it with the Karakoram Highway, which Pakistan and China built further west over the Khunjerab Pass."),
 P("Siachen Glacier", [77.15, 35.42], "A 76 km long glacier in the eastern Karakoram, in Ladakh, one of the longest outside the polar regions. It lies between Pakistan-held Gilgit-Baltistan and the Chinese-held Shaksgam valley, so it guards Ladakh from both. India occupied the Saltoro Ridge above it in Operation Meghdoot in April 1984. At up to about 6,000 m it is the highest battlefield in the world; more soldiers have died here from cold and avalanches than from fighting. The Nubra river, which joins the Shyok, flows out of its snout.", s="Ladakh"),
 P("Khardung La", [77.60, 34.28], "A pass of about 5,360 m just north of Leh, Ladakh, often called one of the highest motorable roads in the world. The road over it links Leh to the Nubra and Shyok valleys, and on to the Siachen base camp, so it is an important army supply route. Tourists ride over it on motorbikes in summer; it closes for long spells in winter."),
 P("Zoji La", [75.47, 34.28], "A pass of about 3,530 m on the Srinagar–Leh road (NH-1). It joins the green Kashmir valley (Sonamarg) to the dry Dras and Kargil area of Ladakh, so it was the key to the Kargil war of 1999. Snow closes it for months, cutting Ladakh off by road. The Zojila tunnel (about 13 km) is being built under it, and the Z-Morh tunnel near Sonamarg on the same road opened in January 2025. Dras, just beyond it, is one of the coldest inhabited places in India."),
 P("Banihal Pass", [75.20, 33.48], "A pass in the Pir Panjal range on the Jammu–Srinagar road, the gateway into the Kashmir valley from the south. The Jawahar tunnel (1956) was the first road tunnel under it. Today the Banihal–Qazigund road tunnel and the 11 km Pir Panjal railway tunnel (one of India's longest) carry traffic below the pass, so the valley stays connected in winter."),
 P("Rohtang Pass", [77.25, 32.37], "A pass of about 3,980 m on the Pir Panjal range in Himachal Pradesh, linking the green Kullu valley (Manali) with the dry Lahaul and Spiti valleys. 'Rohtang' is often said to mean 'pile of corpses', for the travellers who died in its storms. The Atal Tunnel (opened October 2020, about 9 km) runs under it and is the world's longest highway tunnel above 10,000 feet. It keeps the Manali–Leh road open for much more of the year."),
 P("Bara Lacha La", [77.42, 32.76], "A pass of about 4,890 m in Lahaul, Himachal Pradesh, on the Manali–Leh highway. Three rivers start near it: the Chandra and Bhaga (which join at Tandi to form the Chenab, one of the five rivers of Punjab) and the Yunam. Suraj Tal lake lies just below it. In the 2021 paper it was paired correctly with Himachal Pradesh.", y="2021 Q80", s="Himachal Pradesh"),
 P("Shipki La", [78.75, 31.83], "A pass in Kinnaur, Himachal Pradesh, on the border with Tibet. The Sutlej river (Langchen Khambab in Tibet) enters India through this gap, so the pass is a natural doorway. It is one of the three official border trade points with China, with Lipulekh and Nathu La. The old Hindustan–Tibet road ran to it from Shimla."),
 P("Mana Pass", [79.42, 31.07], "A high pass (about 5,600 m) in Chamoli, Uttarakhand, north of the Badrinath temple, leading into Tibet. Below it is Mana village, long called 'the last village of India' (now 'the first village', under the Vibrant Villages programme for border villages). The Saraswati river rises near the pass and joins the Alaknanda at Mana. The 2021 paper paired it correctly with Uttarakhand.", y="2021 Q80", s="Uttarakhand"),
 P("Niti Pass", [79.89, 30.97], "A pass of about 5,070 m in Chamoli, Uttarakhand, east of Mana, on an old trade route between Garhwal and Tibet. The Dhauliganga river rises near it and joins the Alaknanda at Vishnuprayag. The Bhotiya people of the Niti valley traded wool, salt and borax across it until 1962, when the war with China closed the border. The Raini village of the Chipko movement (1973, Gaura Devi) lies down this valley."),
 P("Lipulekh Pass", [80.98, 30.23], "A pass in Pithoragarh, Uttarakhand, at the meeting point of India, Nepal and China. Indian pilgrims to Kailash Mansarovar in Tibet take this route, and it is a border trade point with China. In 2020 India opened a road to it; Nepal objected and printed a new map claiming the nearby Kalapani area and Lipulekh, so it is a live India–Nepal dispute."),
 P("Nathu La", [88.83, 27.39], "A pass of about 4,310 m in Sikkim, on the old Silk Road branch between Gangtok and Lhasa. Indian and Chinese troops clashed here in 1967, and India held the pass. It was reopened for border trade in 2006 and is also a second route for the Kailash Mansarovar Yatra (from 2015). 'Nathu' is said to mean 'listening ears'.", s="Sikkim"),
 P("Jelep La", [88.95, 27.33], "A pass in eastern Sikkim, just south-east of Nathu La, on the old Kalimpong–Lhasa trade route through Tibet's Chumbi valley. Younghusband's British army crossed into Tibet near here in 1903–04. In the 2021 paper the pair 'Jelep La – Arunachal Pradesh' was the wrong one: it is in Sikkim.", y="2021 Q80"),
 P("Doklam", [89.00, 27.28], "A small plateau where Bhutan, China (Tibet's Chumbi valley) and India (Sikkim) meet. Bhutan claims it; China claims it too. In June 2017 Indian troops stopped Chinese soldiers from building a road here, and the two sides faced each other for 73 days. India cared because a Chinese road here would look down on the Siliguri Corridor, the narrow 'chicken's neck' that joins the North-East to the rest of India."),
 P("Bum La", [91.95, 27.70], "A pass north of Tawang, Arunachal Pradesh, on the border with Tibet. In 1962 Chinese troops came into Tawang through it. Today it is one of the official Border Personnel Meeting points where Indian and Chinese officers meet on fixed days. The Dalai Lama fled into India near here in 1959.", k=1),
 P("Sela Pass and Tunnel", [92.10, 27.50], "Sela is a pass of about 4,170 m in West Kameng, Arunachal Pradesh, on the only road to Tawang. Snow and fog used to block it for weeks. The Border Roads Organisation (BRO), which builds roads in border areas, cut the Sela Tunnel under it; the Prime Minister opened it in March 2024. It is described as the world's longest twin-lane road tunnel at about 13,000 feet. It lets the army reach Tawang in any weather. The 2021 paper asked about the final blast on this tunnel (January 2022).", y="2021 Q89", s="Arunachal Pradesh"),
 P("Diphu Pass", [97.35, 28.17], "A pass at the far eastern tip of Arunachal Pradesh, near the point where India, China and Myanmar meet. It is on the McMahon Line, the border drawn at the Simla Convention of 1914 between British India and Tibet, which China does not accept."),
 P("Galwan Valley", [78.20, 34.75], "The valley of the Galwan river in eastern Ladakh, which flows from Aksai Chin (held by China) into the Shyok. It lies on the Line of Actual Control (LAC). On 15 June 2020 Indian and Chinese soldiers fought here with clubs and stones; 20 Indian soldiers died, the first deaths on this border since 1975. Colonel Santosh Babu was given the Maha Vir Chakra after his death."),
 P("Pangong Tso", [78.70, 33.75], "A long, narrow lake (about 134 km) at about 4,250 m in eastern Ladakh. Its water is salty, yet it freezes in winter. Roughly one-third lies in India and two-thirds in Tibet, and the Line of Actual Control crosses it. Ridges on its north bank called 'Fingers' were a flashpoint in 2020. 'Tso' means lake in Tibetan."),
]),
dict(id="rivers", part="India", t="Rivers: where they rise, where they end", intro=(
 "A river question in this exam almost always asks one of three things: where a river rises, which way it flows, or what stands on it (a dam, a city, a temple). "
 "India's rivers fall into two families. The Himalayan rivers (Indus, Ganga, Brahmaputra) are fed by snow and glaciers, so they flow all year and carve deep gorges. "
 "The peninsular rivers are fed only by rain, so many shrink in summer. Most of them rise in the Western Ghats and flow east into the Bay of Bengal, building deltas (fan-shaped land made of river mud): the Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri. "
 "Two big ones flow west into the Arabian Sea through rift valleys (long troughs where the land has sunk between faults) and end in estuaries (open river mouths with no delta): the Narmada and the Tapi. Remember that pair; it is the most-asked river fact."), P=[
 P("Indus (source)", [81.30, 31.20], "The Indus (Sindhu, which gave India its name) rises in Tibet near Lake Mansarovar and Mount Kailash. It flows north-west through Ladakh past Leh, then into Gilgit-Baltistan and Pakistan, and ends in the Arabian Sea near Karachi. Its five eastern tributaries, the Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas and Sutlej, give Punjab ('five waters') its name. Under the Indus Waters Treaty of 1960 (World Bank as broker), India got the three eastern rivers and Pakistan the three western ones; India put the treaty 'in abeyance' after the Pahalgam attack of April 2025.", r="Indus"),
 P("Gangotri (Gaumukh)", [79.08, 30.92], "The Bhagirathi, the main head-stream of the Ganga, comes out of the snout of the Gangotri glacier at Gaumukh ('cow's mouth') in Uttarkashi, Uttarakhand. The Ganga is about 2,525 km long, the longest river flowing within India. It is the national river (declared 2008), and the National Mission for Clean Ganga runs the Namami Gange programme. It flows through Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Jharkhand and West Bengal.", r="Ganges"),
 P("Devprayag", [78.60, 30.15], "In Uttarakhand, where the Bhagirathi and the Alaknanda meet; from here the river is called the Ganga. 'Prayag' means a confluence. The five holy confluences of the Alaknanda (Panch Prayag) are, going downstream: Vishnuprayag (Dhauliganga), Nandprayag (Nandakini), Karnaprayag (Pindar), Rudraprayag (Mandakini) and Devprayag (Bhagirathi)."),
 P("Yamunotri", [78.45, 31.01], "The Yamuna rises at the Yamunotri glacier in Uttarkashi, Uttarakhand. It flows past Delhi, Mathura and Agra (the Taj Mahal is on its bank) and joins the Ganga at Prayagraj. Its main tributaries from the south are the Chambal, Betwa and Ken; the Chambal is home to the gharial (a fish-eating crocodile). It is the longest tributary of the Ganga.", r="Yamuna"),
 P("Prayagraj (Sangam)", [81.88, 25.42], "Formerly Allahabad, in Uttar Pradesh. The Ganga and the Yamuna meet here, with the hidden Saraswati of legend, at the Triveni Sangam. The Kumbh Mela is held here every 12 years (the Maha Kumbh of January–February 2025 drew hundreds of millions of pilgrims). The other Kumbh towns are Haridwar (Ganga), Ujjain (Shipra) and Nashik (Godavari)."),
 P("Brahmaputra in India", [95.30, 28.10], "The Brahmaputra rises near Mansarovar in Tibet as the Yarlung Tsangpo and flows east for about 1,600 km. It then makes a great U-turn round the peak of Namcha Barwa, through the deepest gorge on earth, and enters Arunachal Pradesh as the Siang (Dihang). In Assam it becomes the wide, braided Brahmaputra, full of river islands like Majuli. In Bangladesh it is called the Jamuna and joins the Ganga (Padma). In 2024–25 China began a very large dam on the Tsangpo near the bend, which worries India.", r="Brahmaputra"),
 P("Amarkantak", [81.75, 22.68], "A small plateau town in Anuppur, Madhya Pradesh, on the Maikal range, where the Vindhya and Satpura ranges meet. Two rivers rise here and flow in opposite directions: the Narmada west to the Arabian Sea and the Son north-east to join the Ganga near Patna. The Johila also starts here. The 2021 paper asked which river does not rise in the Maikal range (the Tapi, which rises in the Satpura at Multai).", y="2021 Q72"),
 P("Narmada mouth (Bharuch)", [72.98, 21.70], "The Narmada (about 1,312 km) is the largest west-flowing river of the peninsula. From Amarkantak it runs west through a rift valley between the Vindhya (north) and Satpura (south) ranges, through Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra (border) and Gujarat, and enters the Gulf of Khambhat near Bharuch in an estuary, not a delta. On the way it falls over the Dhuandhar falls and cuts the Marble Rocks gorge at Bhedaghat near Jabalpur. It is often called the line between north and south India.", r="Narmada", y="2021 Q72"),
 P("Sardar Sarovar Dam", [73.75, 21.83], "A large concrete dam on the Narmada at Kevadia (Ekta Nagar), Gujarat, the biggest project of the Narmada valley. It brings water to Gujarat and Rajasthan. Its displacement of villagers led to the Narmada Bachao Andolan (Medha Patkar, Baba Amte). The Statue of Unity, Sardar Patel at 182 m, the tallest statue in the world, stands on an island just below it (2018).", r="Narmada"),
 P("Multai (Tapi source)", [78.25, 21.77], "The Tapi (Tapti, about 724 km) rises at Multai in Betul, Madhya Pradesh, on the Satpura range. Like the Narmada, it flows west through its own rift valley, south of the Satpura, through Maharashtra (Khandesh) and Gujarat, and enters the Gulf of Khambhat at Surat in an estuary. The Ukai dam is on it. Its main tributary is the Purna.", r="Tapi", y="2021 Q72"),
 P("Sihawa (Mahanadi source)", [81.93, 20.30], "The Mahanadi ('great river', about 850 km) rises in the hills near Sihawa in Dhamtari, Chhattisgarh. It flows north then east through Chhattisgarh and Odisha and builds a large delta around Cuttack before reaching the Bay of Bengal near Paradip. Chhattisgarh and Odisha share its water and have a dispute about it before a tribunal.", r="Mahanadi"),
 P("Hirakud Dam", [83.87, 21.53], "On the Mahanadi near Sambalpur, Odisha, completed in 1957. Its main earthen dam with dykes is about 25.8 km long, among the longest in the world, and its lake is one of the largest man-made lakes in Asia. It was built to control the floods that used to drown the Mahanadi delta, and it also gives power and irrigation.", r="Mahanadi"),
 P("Trimbakeshwar (Godavari source)", [73.53, 19.93], "The Godavari (about 1,465 km) rises at Trimbakeshwar near Nashik, Maharashtra, in the Western Ghats. It is the longest river of peninsular India and the second longest in India, so it is called the Dakshin Ganga (Ganga of the south). It flows east through Maharashtra, Telangana and Andhra Pradesh and builds a big delta near Rajahmundry. The Polavaram project is on it.", r="Godavari"),
 P("Mahabaleshwar (Krishna source)", [73.66, 17.92], "The Krishna (about 1,400 km) rises near Mahabaleshwar in the Western Ghats, Maharashtra. It flows east through Maharashtra, Karnataka, Telangana and Andhra Pradesh to the Bay of Bengal, building a delta near Vijayawada. Its main tributaries are the Bhima, Tungabhadra, Koyna and Ghataprabha. The Koyna dam on its tributary sits in a quake-prone area.", r="Krishna"),
 P("Nagarjuna Sagar Dam", [79.31, 16.57], "A large masonry (stone) dam on the Krishna on the Telangana–Andhra Pradesh border, built 1955–67. It is named after the Buddhist teacher Nagarjuna; the remains of the old Buddhist centre of Nagarjunakonda were moved to an island in its lake. The Srisailam dam is upstream on the same river."),
 P("Talakaveri (Kaveri source)", [75.49, 12.39], "The Kaveri (Cauvery, about 800 km) rises at Talakaveri on the Brahmagiri hills in Kodagu (Coorg), Karnataka. It flows south-east through Karnataka (Krishnaraja Sagar dam near Mysuru, Shivanasamudra falls) and Tamil Nadu (Mettur dam), and builds a wide, rich delta around Thanjavur, the rice bowl of Tamil Nadu. Karnataka and Tamil Nadu fought over its water for decades; the Cauvery Water Management Authority now shares it.", r="Kaveri"),
 P("Bhakra Dam", [76.43, 31.41], "A tall concrete gravity dam on the Sutlej in Bilaspur, Himachal Pradesh, finished in 1963. Its lake is the Gobind Sagar. With the Nangal dam below it, it irrigates Punjab, Haryana and Rajasthan and helped the Green Revolution. Nehru called such dams 'the new temples of resurgent India'."),
 P("Tehri Dam", [78.48, 30.38], "A rock-and-earth fill dam on the Bhagirathi near Tehri, Uttarakhand (about 260 m), the tallest dam in India. It supplies power and drinking water to Delhi and Uttar Pradesh. The old Tehri town was drowned under its lake, and the Chipko leader Sunderlal Bahuguna fought against it because the area is prone to earthquakes."),
]),
dict(id="relief", part="India", t="Mountains, plateaus and deserts", intro=(
 "India's land has three big parts. In the north is the Himalaya, young fold mountains (rock layers pushed up into folds when the Indian plate pressed into Asia), still rising and full of earthquakes. "
 "In the middle are the flat Northern Plains, made of silt (fine river mud) laid down by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra. "
 "In the south is the Peninsular Plateau, made of very old, hard rock, part of the ancient land of Gondwana. Ranges ring it: the Aravalli (the oldest fold mountains in India) in the north-west, the Vindhya and Satpura across the middle, and the Western and Eastern Ghats along the two coasts. "
 "For each range, learn its highest point. For each plateau, learn what it is rich in."), P=[
 P("K2 (Godwin-Austen)", [76.51, 35.88], "At 8,611 m, K2 is the second highest peak in the world, in the Karakoram range. It stands in Gilgit-Baltistan, which India counts as part of Ladakh but Pakistan holds, so Indian books call it the highest peak of India. The name K2 comes from 'Karakoram peak number 2' in a British survey of 1856; it is also named Godwin-Austen after a surveyor. It is considered harder and deadlier to climb than Everest."),
 P("Kanchenjunga", [88.15, 27.70], "At 8,586 m, the third highest peak in the world, on the border of Sikkim and Nepal. It is the highest peak within India's present borders and is sacred to the people of Sikkim. The Khangchendzonga National Park around it became India's first 'mixed' World Heritage Site (natural and cultural) in 2016.", s="Sikkim"),
 P("Nanda Devi", [79.97, 30.38], "At 7,816 m, the highest peak that lies wholly inside India, in Chamoli, Uttarakhand. It is surrounded by a ring of peaks called the Nanda Devi Sanctuary. The Nanda Devi and Valley of Flowers National Parks together are a World Heritage Site. A nuclear-powered spying device was lost on it in 1965 in a secret Indo-US mission."),
 P("Guru Shikhar", [72.78, 24.65], "At 1,722 m, the highest point of the Aravalli range, on Mount Abu, Rajasthan (Rajasthan's only hill station; the Dilwara Jain temples are there). The Aravalli run about 700 km from Gujarat to Delhi and are the oldest fold mountains in India, worn down to low hills. They lie parallel to the monsoon winds, so they do not stop rain, which is one reason the Thar is dry to their west."),
 P("Vindhya Range", [78.00, 23.70], "An old, low range across central India north of the Narmada, from Gujarat to Bihar. It forms the southern edge of the Malwa plateau. For centuries it was the line between 'north' (Aryavarta) and 'south' (Dakshinapatha). Its sandstone was used for many monuments, including the Red Fort and Sanchi."),
 P("Satpura Range", [77.50, 21.90], "A range across central India between the Narmada (north) and the Tapi (south), so the Narmada flows in a trough between the Vindhya and the Satpura. Its highest point is Dhupgarh (1,350 m) near Pachmarhi, Madhya Pradesh's only hill station. The Satpura Tiger Reserve is here. In the east it joins the Maikal range at Amarkantak."),
 P("Western Ghats (Sahyadri)", [73.80, 17.50], "A steep wall of hills about 1,600 km long along the west coast, from the Tapi in Gujarat to Kanyakumari. They block the south-west monsoon winds, so the coast and the slopes get heavy rain while the land behind them (the Deccan) is in a rain shadow and much drier. Most peninsular rivers rise here and flow east. They are a UNESCO World Heritage Site (2012) and, with Sri Lanka, one of the world's 36 biodiversity hotspots (areas very rich in species that are under threat). Gaps: Thal Ghat, Bhor Ghat, Pal Ghat."),
 P("Anamudi", [77.06, 10.17], "At 2,695 m, the highest peak of the Western Ghats and of all India south of the Himalaya, in the Anaimalai hills of Idukki, Kerala. The name means 'elephant's forehead'. It is in Eravikulam National Park, home of the Nilgiri tahr (a mountain goat)."),
 P("Doddabetta", [76.73, 11.40], "At 2,637 m, the highest peak of the Nilgiri hills, near Ooty (Udhagamandalam), Tamil Nadu. The Nilgiris ('blue mountains', from the blue flowers of the neelakurinji that bloom every 12 years) are the knot where the Western and Eastern Ghats meet. The Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve (1986) was India's first."),
 P("Eastern Ghats", [82.50, 18.00], "A broken chain of old hills along the east coast, from Odisha to Tamil Nadu. They are lower and more broken than the Western Ghats because the big east-flowing rivers (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) cut through them. Their highest peak is Jindhagada (about 1,690 m) in Andhra Pradesh; Mahendragiri in Odisha is a well-known peak. Rich in bauxite (aluminium ore).", k=1),
 P("Deccan Plateau", [77.50, 17.20], "The big triangular plateau of Maharashtra and the south, sloping gently east. Its north-west part is the Deccan Traps: layers of basalt lava that poured out about 66 million years ago. That basalt breaks down into black soil (regur), which holds water and is ideal for cotton, so it is called black cotton soil."),
 P("Chota Nagpur Plateau", [85.00, 23.50], "A plateau in Jharkhand and parts of Odisha, Chhattisgarh and West Bengal, called the mineral heartland of India: coal (Jharia, Bokaro), iron ore, copper, mica, bauxite and uranium (Jaduguda). It drew India's first steel plant to Jamshedpur (Tata, 1907). Ranchi stands on it; the Damodar valley, once the 'sorrow of Bengal' for its floods, runs along it."),
 P("Shillong (Meghalaya) Plateau", [91.50, 25.50], "A block of old rock made of the Garo, Khasi and Jaintia hills, the three main tribes of Meghalaya. It is a piece of the Peninsular plateau, cut off from the main block by the low land of the Malda gap. Its south face catches the monsoon winds coming up from the Bay of Bengal, which is why it is so wet. Living root bridges and limestone caves are found here."),
 P("Mawsynram", [91.58, 25.30], "A village in the Khasi hills, Meghalaya, recorded as one of the wettest places on earth (about 11,800 mm of rain a year). Nearby Cherrapunji (Sohra) held the record before. They are so wet because moist monsoon winds from the Bay of Bengal are funnelled into a valley and forced to rise up the steep plateau, so they drop their rain.", s="Meghalaya"),
 P("Thar Desert", [71.00, 27.00], "The Great Indian Desert, mainly in western Rajasthan, with parts in Gujarat, Haryana and Pakistan. It gets under about 250 mm of rain a year. It is dry because the monsoon winds from the Arabian Sea pass along the Aravalli, which run parallel to them, so nothing forces the winds to rise and drop rain. The Indira Gandhi Canal brings Himalayan water to it. Animals: the great Indian bustard (critically endangered) and the blackbuck; Desert National Park is near Jaisalmer."),
]),
]
